[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, by Hilbert's Nullstellensatz (the weak form), since $f_1, \ldots, f_m$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhave no common zero in $\mathbb{C}^n$, the ideal $(f_1, \ldots, f_m)$ in $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{C}[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$ is the unit ideal, i.e., $1 \in (f_1, \ldots, f_m)[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\subset \mathbb{C}[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$. So there exist $h_1, \ldots, h_m \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{C}[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$ with $1 = \sum f_i h_i$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is whether we can find such $g_i$ with coefficients in $\mathbb{Q}$[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(in fact in $\mathbb{Q}$, not just $\mathbb{C}$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $f_i \in \mathbb{Z}[x_1, \ldots, x_n] \subset \mathbb{Q}[x_1, \ldots, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx_n]$, we're asking: if the ideal generated by $f_1, \ldots, f_m$ in $\mathbb{C}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$ is the unit ideal, is the ideal generated by $f_1, \ldots, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mf_m$ in $\mathbb{Q}[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$ also the unit ideal?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a question about faithful flatness / field extension properties.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey fact: If $k \subset K$ is a field extension and $I$ is an ideal in $k[x_1, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\ldots, x_n]$, then $I \cdot K[x_1, \ldots, x_n] \cap k[x_1, \ldots, x_n] = I$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhen $K$ is faithfully flat over $k$. Actually, every field extension is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfaithfully flat (since fields are flat over subfields, and faithfully flat [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbecause $K \neq 0$ as a $k$-module... actually let me think more carefully).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the key fact is: $K[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$ is a faithfully flat $k[x_1, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\ldots, x_n]$-algebra when $K/k$ is a field extension. This is because $K[x_1, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\ldots, x_n] \cong K \otimes_k k[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$, and $K$ is faithfully flat [0m[38;2;124;124;124mover $k$ (any nonzero module over a field is free, hence flat; and $K \neq 0$ so[0m[38;2;124;124;124mit's faithfully flat). The tensor product of faithfully flat modules is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfaithfully flat.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo if $I \cdot K[x_1, \ldots, x_n] = K[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$ (i.e., the extended [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mideal is the unit ideal), then $I = k[x_1, \ldots, x_n]$ (the original ideal is [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe unit ideal).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me be more careful. Faithfully flat means: $M = 0$ iff $M \otimes K = [0m[38;2;124;124;124m0$, and more generally, a sequence is exact iff its tensor with $K$ is exact.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor ideals: If $A \to B$ is faithfully flat, and $I$ is an ideal of $A$, then [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$IB \cap A = I$. In particular, $IB = B$ iff $I = A$.[0m

